@style(IDocDemo)
@title(Code 2: every @@code argument)

Each block shows one argument of @@code at work.

@section(Language, size and text)

`language=` names the language when it is not the first argument; `width=` and `height=` size the editor; `fs=` sets the code's text size.

@code(language=python, run, width=60%, height=6, fs=11)
print("sized editor")
@end

`caption=` puts a caption under the listing and `label=` lets @@ref point at it.

@code(python, caption=A loop that counts, label=lst:count)
for i in range(3):
    print(i)
@end

The listing above is @ref(lst:count).

`text=` gives the code inline when it is not the first argument: @code(python, text=print(1)).

@section(Running)

`stdin=` feeds input to the program; `timeout=` stops it after that many seconds.

@code(python, run, stdin="4", timeout=5)
n = int(input())
print(n * n)
@end

`active=` lists the lines a student may edit; the rest stay locked.

@code(python, run, active=2)
total = 0
total = total + 5
print(total)
@end

`scaffold` locks everything except the region between `@@edit` and `@@end` comment markers.

@code(python, run, scaffold)
import statistics
# @edit
def mean(xs):
    return sum(xs) / len(xs)
# @end
print(mean([1, 2, 3]))
@end

`benchmark=` names a timer that receives the run time in milliseconds, shown in the toolbar.

@code(python, run, benchmark=loop_ms)
print(sum(range(100000)))
@end

@section(Marking)

`expect=` is the output the program must print; `tol=` lets a printed number be close.

@code(python, run, expect="15")
print(sum(range(6)))
@end

@code(python, run, expect="3.1416", tol=0.001)
import math
print(round(math.pi, 4))
@end

A @@tests section holds hidden checks; each line is one check, and the score is the fraction that pass. `pts=` sets the points and `debug` shows the result of each test.

@code(python, run, pts=3, debug)
def add(a, b):
    return a + b
@tests
assert add(2, 3) == 5
assert add(-1, 1) == 0
assert add(0, 0) == 0
@end

A test line can carry its own input after `<<<`; with an @@solution, the expected output comes from the teacher's program.

@code(python, run, pts=2)
n = int(input())
print(n * 2)
@tests
<<< 5
<<< 11
@solution
n = int(input())
print(n * 2)
@end

`tests=` can name a file of checks instead, such as a Verilog testbench compiled with the student's module.

@code(systemverilog, run, pts=4, tests=02-arguments.assets/and_tb.sv)
module and2(input logic a, b, output logic y);
  assign y = a & b;
endmodule
@end

`id=` names the exercise (its mark and draft hang off it); `submit=server` makes it a hand-in; `time=` limits how long it stays open in a live class; `points=` is another spelling of `pts=`.

@code(python, run, id=ex_double, points=2, submit=server, time=5m, expect="10")
print(5 * 2)
@end

`lines=` sets the ruled lines for a handwritten answer when the paper is printed as a scan form.

@code(python, edit, lines=10, pts=2)
# write a function that reverses a list
@end

`file=` shows and runs a source file instead of the body.

@code(file=02-arguments.assets/hello.c, run)
@end
